            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest NASA/JPL news about the Mars Perseverance rover is that it captured a first-of-its-kind view from Mars: Earth disappearing behind the Martian moon Phobos. The images were taken by Perseverance’s Mastcam-Z instrument on July 2, 2026, the rover’s 1,907th Martian day, and NASA published the story on Aug. 5, 2026. NASA says this is the first time humanity has captured Earth being occulted by another object from the surface of another planet【5:1†source】.\n\nA few key details:\n\n- Earth appears as a tiny bright dot in the Martian sky, while Phobos crosses in front of it, making Earth briefly “wink out” behind the moon’s shadowed edge【5:1†source】.\n- The observation was made around 7 p.m. local solar time at Perseverance’s location on the rim of Jezero Crater【5:1†source】.\n- NASA described the image as a “unique Earth self-portrait” from another planet, with a “Phobos photobomb”【5:1†source】.\n- Perseverance is still actively returning images; NASA’s rover image page shows an “Image of the Week” for Week 285, covering July 26–Aug. 1, 2026【5:2†source】.\n\nRecent science news from the mission also includes Perseverance’s investigation of the “Broom Point member,” a 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of ancient rock on the rim of Jezero Crater. NASA reported in July 2026 that the rover found evidence this rock sequence was built by repeated asteroid impacts, offering clues about very ancient Martian geology【5:4†source】.",
                "annotations": [
